<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Karlowitz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1699">1699</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sremski-karlovci">Karlowitz</a> was signed in 1699 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, ending the war that followed the failed Ottoman siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683. The Ottomans surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire treat it as the first major permanent territorial loss in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and a marker of the empire's decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz.jpg" alt="Treaty of Karlowitz" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: O.Mustafin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Karlowitz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Holy League retook Buda in 1686, and Prince Eugene of Savoy destroyed the Ottoman army at the Battle of Zenta in 1697, leading to the <b>Treaty of Karlowitz</b> in 1699.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Karlowitz</b> in 1699, which surrendered most of Hungary and Transylvania to the Habsburgs and Podolia to Poland, and the Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca in 1774, which made the Crimean Khanate independent and gave Russia provisions it later interpreted as a right to protect the sultan’s…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its principal danger came from Venice, which sought to dominate the Adriatic, and the <b>Treaty of Karlowitz</b> of 1699 left two narrow strips of Ottoman territory at Neum and Sutorina, separating Ragusa from Venetian Dalmatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Edirne Incident, a janissary revolt against the consequences of the <b>Treaty of Karlowitz</b> and the sultan's absence from the capital, Mustafa II was deposed and his brother Ahmed III became sultan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Holy League and the Ottoman Empire concluded the <b>Treaty of Karlowitz</b>, ending the war that had begun with the failed Ottoman siege of Vienna in 1683.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory led to the <b>Treaty of Karlowitz</b> of 1699.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-11">11 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
